<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#propiska-in-the-soviet-union">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Propiska</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as propiski</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A propiska was a written residency permit and migration record used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> before 1917 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1932 until 1991, entered as a stamp in a citizen's internal passport. Passports were abolished after the October Revolution, but in December 1932 Stalin's government re-established a unified internal passport, above all to stop peasants flooding to the towns in the famine of 1930–1933; peasants could obtain passports only by special application. A permanent propiska confirmed the right to housing and was nearly impossible to revoke, while settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> or Leningrad without one was very hard. The system was ruled illegal and abolished on 11 October 1991.</p></div>
